package com.hpcai270

// Days 26–45: storage fundamentals → performance → HPC storage.
val hpcDaysPart5 = listOf(
    Day(26,"Storage protocols","Block, file and object","Storage is exposed through different interfaces. Block gives a device-like volume, file gives a shared namespace, and object gives API-addressed objects with metadata.","MENTAL MODEL\n\nBLOCK → volume/device\nFILE → path/filesystem\nOBJECT → object + metadata + API\n\nExamples: FC, iSCSI, NVMe-oF • NFS, SMB, Lustre • S3.","REAL WORLD\n\nA SAN presents block storage. A NAS presents files. Cloudian provides S3-compatible object storage. Lustre and IBM Storage Scale provide parallel filesystems for HPC workloads.","WHY HPC IS DIFFERENT\n\nAI training can have hundreds of workers reading many files at once. A protocol and filesystem must support concurrency, throughput and predictable latency without becoming the bottleneck.","STORAGE-ADMIN CONNECTION\n\nYour existing SAN/NAS knowledge is useful. The key upgrade is understanding why HPC adds parallel filesystems, NVMe, high-speed fabrics and aggressive benchmarking.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nFirst identify the interface: block, file or object. Then identify the path: client → network → protocol → storage service → media. Troubleshoot the correct layer instead of treating every storage problem as an array problem.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: make a three-column table in your notebook for block/file/object and list protocol, example product and common workload.\n\nInterview: ‘Why would an AI training platform use a parallel filesystem instead of ordinary NAS?’\n\nYOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO: classify a storage architecture before troubleshooting it."),

    Day(27,"NFS","Shared files over IP","NFS lets Linux clients access files exported by a server. It is foundational knowledge for HPC because many environments use NFS for management, home directories, tools or less performance-sensitive data.","PATH\n\nAPPLICATION → VFS → NFS CLIENT → TCP/IP → NFS SERVER → FILESYSTEM → DISK\n\nEvery arrow is a possible fault domain.","ANALOGY\n\nA shared office filing room: many people can access the same folders, but the speed depends on the room, hallway, access rules and filing system.","IMPORTANT IDEAS\n\nExport = server-side share.\nMount = client-side attachment.\nUID/GID = identity.\nNFS version and mount options affect behavior.\n\nDo not confuse a mounted path with local storage.","COMMANDS\n\nfindmnt\nmount\ndf -hT\nshowmount -e <server>\n\nFor a lab, export a directory from one Linux VM and mount it from another.","PERFORMANCE\n\nNFS performance depends on client behavior, network latency/bandwidth, server CPU, filesystem, RPC behavior and backend storage. A fast array cannot compensate for a poor network path.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nMount failure → DNS/IP, route, firewall, export, permissions.\nSlow I/O → network, server load, filesystem, client behavior, backend latency.\nPermission problem → UID/GID/ownership/export policy.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: build two Linux VMs, export /labshare and mount it on the client. Create a file from the client and verify it on the server.\n\nInterview: ‘Walk me through an NFS read from application to disk.’\n\nNEXT LINK: object storage uses a very different API model."),

    Day(28,"S3 and object storage","Objects, buckets and APIs","Object storage stores objects addressed through an API rather than presenting a traditional POSIX filesystem path. S3 compatibility is extremely important in modern AI and data platforms.","MODEL\n\nAPPLICATION\n ↓\nS3 API\n ↓\nBUCKET\n ↓\nOBJECT + METADATA\n ↓\nOBJECT STORAGE CLUSTER\n\nThe application asks for objects rather than issuing ordinary filesystem operations.","ANALOGY\n\nA parcel warehouse: each package has an identifier and metadata. You request a package by its identifier rather than walking through a filesystem directory tree.","COMMON TERMS\n\nBucket = logical container.\nObject = data plus metadata.\nKey = object identifier.\nEndpoint = service address.\n\nCloudian HyperStore is an example of enterprise S3-compatible object storage.","AI USE\n\nObject storage is useful for datasets, raw training data, archives and data lakes. AI pipelines may copy or stage data into faster filesystems before training.","PERFORMANCE\n\nObject workloads can be highly parallel, but application access patterns and object size matter. Object storage is not automatically a drop-in replacement for a low-latency POSIX filesystem.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nCheck endpoint connectivity, credentials/authorization, bucket policy, object key, request errors and service health. Separate API failures from backend capacity or performance problems.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: using a local S3-compatible lab if available, create a bucket and upload/download an object. If no object service is available, draw the API path.\n\nInterview: ‘Why is S3 not the same thing as mounting NFS?’\n\nYOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO: explain bucket/object/key and where object storage fits in an AI pipeline."),

    Day(29,"RAID and data protection","Performance versus resilience","RAID combines drives to provide capacity, performance and/or fault tolerance. Modern distributed storage often uses additional replication or erasure coding, but RAID remains important for understanding the physical storage layer.","BASIC LEVELS\n\nRAID 0 → striping, no redundancy\nRAID 1 → mirroring\nRAID 5/6 → parity\nRAID 10 → mirrored pairs + striping\n\nExact usable capacity depends on layout and drive count.","ANALOGY\n\nRAID 0 is several checkout lanes sharing work. RAID 1 keeps a duplicate receipt. Parity RAID stores recovery information so lost data can be reconstructed.","WRITE PENALTY\n\nParity RAID can require extra reads/calculations/writes for some small-write patterns. RAID level therefore affects workload performance, not only protection.","HPC CONNECTION\n\nHPC systems can stack protection layers: drive-level protection, storage-server redundancy, distributed replication/erasure coding and filesystem-level behavior. Understand which layer owns which failure.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nA degraded array may remain available but have reduced performance. Rebuild activity can consume I/O bandwidth. Always distinguish media failure, controller failure, filesystem issue and application symptom.","TOOLS\n\nIn Linux labs inspect block devices with lsblk and software RAID with mdadm status where applicable. Enterprise arrays expose their own health and performance telemetry.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: draw RAID 0, 1, 5, 6 and 10 with four drives. Mark usable capacity and what happens after one drive fails.\n\nInterview: ‘Why can a healthy RAID array still deliver poor application performance?’\n\nNEXT LINK: tomorrow we quantify performance instead of saying ‘fast’ or ‘slow’."),

    Day(30,"Storage performance metrics","IOPS, throughput and latency","Storage performance must be measured with defined metrics. The three most important starting points are IOPS, throughput and latency.","THE THREE\n\nIOPS = I/O operations per second\nTHROUGHPUT = bytes per second\nLATENCY = time required for an operation\n\nThey describe different aspects of the same system.","ANALOGY\n\nA highway: throughput is how many vehicles pass per hour; latency is how long one vehicle takes; IOPS resembles how many individual transactions are completed.","WHY WORKLOAD MATTERS\n\n4 KiB random reads and 1 MiB sequential reads can produce radically different results on the same storage. Block size, read/write mix, queue depth and concurrency must be specified.","AI EXAMPLE\n\nTraining can demand high aggregate throughput when many workers stream data. Checkpointing can create large sequential writes. Metadata-heavy workloads stress different resources.","LATENCY BUDGET\n\nAPPLICATION → FILESYSTEM → NETWORK → STORAGE SERVICE → MEDIA\n\nThe end-to-end latency is influenced by each layer. Optimizing one layer may not improve application latency if another layer dominates.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nNever report ‘storage is slow’ without a metric. Ask: latency at what layer? throughput with what block size? IOPS at what queue depth? from how many clients?\n\nMethodology is part of the result.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: write down a benchmark specification: 4 KiB random read, 70/30 mix, 8 jobs, queue depth 32. Explain why each parameter matters.\n\nInterview: ‘When would throughput matter more than IOPS?’\n\nYOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO: define a storage workload before measuring it."),

    Day(31,"FIO","Your storage microscope","FIO is a flexible I/O workload generator used to test storage behavior. It can create controlled reads and writes with selectable block sizes, queue depths, jobs and access patterns.","WORKLOAD MODEL\n\nFIO JOB\n ├─ read/write pattern\n ├─ block size\n ├─ number of jobs\n ├─ queue depth\n ├─ target file/device\n └─ runtime\n\nThe configuration is the experiment.","KEY TERMS\n\nbs = block size\nrw = read/write pattern\nnumjobs = concurrent workers\niodepth = outstanding I/O per worker\nruntime = test duration\n\nChange one variable at a time when learning cause and effect.","EXAMPLE CONCEPT\n\nfio --name=test --filename=/path/testfile --rw=read --bs=1M --size=4G --runtime=60 --time_based\n\nTreat commands as lab examples. Never point destructive write tests at production storage.","READ THE OUTPUT\n\nLook for bandwidth, IOPS and latency percentiles. Do not focus only on the headline number. Tail latency can matter greatly to applications.","COMMON MISTAKES\n\nTesting a tiny file that fits in cache.\nTesting an unrepresentative block size.\nIgnoring filesystem/network overhead.\nComparing two tests with different queue depth.\n\nA benchmark is only useful when the methodology is controlled.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: install FIO in a Linux VM and run a safe read test against a test file. Record IOPS, bandwidth and latency.\n\nInterview: ‘What does queue depth change in an FIO test?’\n\nNEXT LINK: concurrency and queue depth are central to HPC performance."),

    Day(32,"Queue depth and concurrency","Keeping the pipeline busy","Queue depth represents outstanding I/O requests. Concurrency determines how many workers or operations can be active. HPC systems often need substantial concurrency to expose storage parallelism.","PIPELINE\n\nAPPLICATIONS → REQUEST QUEUE → NETWORK → STORAGE QUEUES → MEDIA\n\nToo little concurrency can underutilize the system. Too much can create contention and tail latency.","ANALOGY\n\nOne checkout lane cannot use a supermarket's full capacity. Opening more lanes increases throughput—until the aisles or cashiers become congested.","FIO CONNECTION\n\nnumjobs increases parallel workers. iodepth increases outstanding operations per worker. Their interaction changes workload pressure on the stack.","HPC CONNECTION\n\nHundreds of compute nodes can generate enormous concurrent I/O. A parallel filesystem is designed to distribute work across servers and storage targets rather than funneling everything through one path.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nLow throughput at QD1 may be latency-bound. Increasing QD can improve throughput but eventually saturate a bottleneck. If latency explodes while throughput stops improving, you may have crossed the useful operating point.","EXPERIMENT\n\nRun the same safe workload at QD 1, 4, 16 and 32. Plot throughput and latency. Identify the point where additional queue depth stops helping.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nInterview: ‘Why might increasing queue depth make an application slower?’\n\nYOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO: explain concurrency as a resource and identify saturation from benchmark results."),

    Day(33,"Latency","The time hidden inside every I/O","Latency is the elapsed time for an operation. In distributed storage, end-to-end latency includes software, network and storage components.","LATENCY CHAIN\n\nSYSTEM CALL\n ↓\nVFS/FILESYSTEM\n ↓\nNETWORK\n ↓\nSTORAGE SERVICE\n ↓\nDEVICE\n ↓\nRESPONSE\n\nThe application sees the combined path.","ANALOGY\n\nA restaurant order can be delayed by the waiter, kitchen, oven or delivery route. Knowing only the total delay does not identify the slow stage.","AVERAGES ARE NOT ENOUGH\n\nMedian latency describes the typical request. High percentiles such as p95/p99 expose tail behavior. Distributed systems can have small numbers of very slow operations that affect applications.","AI CONNECTION\n\nTraining jobs synchronize across workers. A subset of slow I/O operations can cause workers to wait, reducing effective GPU utilization.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nCheck whether latency rises with queue depth, concurrency, network load or backend contention. Compare client-observed latency with storage-side metrics when available.","TOOLS\n\nFIO reports latency distributions. Linux tools such as iostat can show device-level behavior. Network tools help separate transport problems from storage media problems.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: run a safe FIO test and record average plus percentile latency. Explain why p99 could matter even if average latency looks good.\n\nInterview: ‘Why is tail latency important in distributed AI workloads?’\n\nNEXT LINK: throughput measures how much data the system can move."),

    Day(34,"Throughput","Moving data at scale","Throughput is the rate of data transfer, usually measured in MB/s or GB/s. HPC storage often emphasizes aggregate throughput because many workers may read or write data concurrently.","AGGREGATION\n\nCLIENT 1 ─┐\nCLIENT 2 ─┼→ FABRIC → STORAGE → GB/s\nCLIENT 3 ─┤\n...       ┘\n\nAggregate bandwidth can be much larger than a single client's result.","ANALOGY\n\nA water pipeline's capacity is its flow rate. More pipes can increase total flow, but only until a shared valve or reservoir becomes the bottleneck.","WHAT LIMITS THROUGHPUT?\n\nMedia bandwidth\nStorage servers\nFilesystem layout\nNetwork links\nProtocol overhead\nClient CPU/memory\nApplication request pattern\n\nThe slowest shared component often dominates.","AI EXAMPLE\n\nIf 128 GPUs each need data at high rate, storage must provide aggregate bandwidth without starving workers. This is why AI infrastructure uses parallel filesystems and high-speed networking.","BENCHMARKING\n\nSpecify number of clients, block size, read/write mix, concurrency and test duration. Compare both per-client and aggregate results.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nIf one client is fast but 100 clients are slow, suspect shared bottlenecks, contention or architecture—not necessarily the individual storage device.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: calculate aggregate bandwidth from ten clients each achieving 2 GB/s. Then list three shared bottlenecks that could prevent linear scaling.\n\nInterview: ‘Why doesn't 10 × client bandwidth always equal system bandwidth?’\n\nYOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO: reason about scaling and shared bottlenecks."),

    Day(35,"HPC storage architecture","From clients to parallel storage","HPC storage separates compute from shared data infrastructure so many compute nodes can access large datasets. The architecture is designed around parallelism, bandwidth and resilience.","REFERENCE ARCHITECTURE\n\nCOMPUTE NODES\n   ↓\nHIGH-SPEED FABRIC\n   ↓\nPARALLEL FILESYSTEM\n   ├─ METADATA SERVICES\n   └─ STORAGE SERVICES\n          ↓\n       NVMe / SSD / HDD\n\nManagement and object/archive tiers may coexist.","ANALOGY\n\nA logistics center has many workers, many loading docks and separate catalog staff. The catalog system tracks where items are; loading docks move the bulk goods.","WHY PARALLEL?\n\nOne storage server cannot always deliver the required aggregate bandwidth. Parallel filesystems distribute metadata and data operations across multiple servers and targets.","REAL PRODUCTS\n\nDDN EXAScaler → Lustre-based HPC/AI filesystem.\nIBM Storage Scale → parallel filesystem/data platform evolved from GPFS.\nWEKA and VAST → modern high-performance data platforms.\nDell PowerScale → scale-out file storage used in some AI/HPC environments.","STORAGE ADMIN CONNECTION\n\nYour enterprise storage experience transfers to capacity, protection, health and performance—but HPC adds distributed filesystem behavior, high-speed networking and workload benchmarking.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nStart at the application symptom, then trace client → fabric → filesystem → metadata/data service → device. Ask whether the issue affects one client, one server, one target or the whole cluster.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: draw this architecture and label the control/metadata path versus the bulk data path.\n\nInterview: ‘Why does HPC storage need multiple storage servers?’\n\nNEXT LINK: tomorrow we open the most important HPC parallel filesystem—Lustre."),

    Day(36,"Lustre architecture","MDS, OSS and clients","Lustre is a parallel distributed filesystem widely used in HPC. Its architecture separates metadata functions from bulk file data so many clients can access storage targets concurrently.","CORE COMPONENTS\n\nCLIENTS → Lustre filesystem\nMDS/MDT → metadata\nOSS/OST → file data\n\nSimplified:\nCLIENT → MDS for metadata\nCLIENT → OSS/OST for data\n\nExact deployments can include redundancy and multiple services.","ANALOGY\n\nThe MDS is the librarian who tells you which shelves contain a book. OSS/OST are the shelves. Clients are readers. Separating the catalog from the shelves lets many readers retrieve data in parallel.","METADATA VS DATA\n\nCreate/open/stat/rename operations are metadata-heavy. Large sequential reads/writes are data-heavy. These can stress different parts of the system.","WHY AI LIKES IT\n\nMany compute nodes can read datasets and write checkpoints concurrently. Lustre can stripe files across multiple OSTs to increase aggregate bandwidth.","REAL WORLD\n\nDDN EXAScaler is a commercial Lustre-based storage platform used for HPC and AI workloads. Lustre is also deployed in large research and supercomputing environments.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nIf file operations are slow but bulk I/O is healthy, investigate metadata services. If large reads are slow, investigate clients, network, OSS/OST, striping and media. Always identify the workload type.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: install Lustre only in a supported lab environment or use a vendor/community lab. Draw MDS, MDT, OSS, OST and client relationships.\n\nInterview: ‘What is the difference between metadata traffic and data traffic in Lustre?’\n\nNEXT LINK: tomorrow we mount Lustre clients and inspect the client path."),

    Day(37,"Lustre clients and mounts","Making the filesystem usable","A Lustre client is the compute-side component that mounts and accesses a Lustre filesystem. Understanding the client path is critical because many apparent storage faults begin at the client.","PATH\n\nAPPLICATION\n ↓\nPOSIX/VFS\n ↓\nLUSTRE CLIENT\n ↓\nLNET / NETWORK\n ↓\nMDS or OSS\n ↓\nMDT/OST\n\nDifferent operations take different server paths.","ANALOGY\n\nA customer enters a store through a front desk, then reaches either the information counter or merchandise area. The client is the entry point into the distributed filesystem.","CLIENT RESPONSIBILITIES\n\nMount filesystem\nMaintain client state\nIssue metadata/data requests\nUse network transport\nCache some information\n\nClient configuration can therefore affect performance.","COMMON OBSERVATIONS\n\nMounted but slow.\nMount fails.\nOne client differs from others.\nAll clients slow.\n\nThese symptoms imply different fault domains.","TROUBLESHOOTING METHOD\n\n1. Verify mount.\n2. Verify client logs.\n3. Verify network/fabric.\n4. Compare another client.\n5. Check MDS/OSS health.\n6. Benchmark a controlled workload.\n\nDo not change multiple variables simultaneously.","TOOLS\n\nfindmnt\nmount\ndmesg/journalctl\nLustre-specific client commands depend on the Lustre release and deployment. Learn the exact commands from the environment's documentation.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: in a Lustre lab, mount the filesystem and create/read a test file. Record mount options and identify which server handles metadata versus data.\n\nInterview: ‘How would you decide whether a Lustre problem is client-specific?’\n\nNEXT LINK: striping determines where file data is distributed."),

    Day(38,"Lustre striping","Splitting files across targets","Lustre striping distributes file data across multiple Object Storage Targets. Striping can increase parallel bandwidth because different portions of a file can be serviced concurrently.","CONCEPT\n\nFILE\n ├─ stripe 0 → OST 0\n ├─ stripe 1 → OST 1\n ├─ stripe 2 → OST 2\n └─ stripe 3 → OST 3\n\nThe exact layout is controlled by filesystem settings and file-level striping parameters.","ANALOGY\n\nInstead of one cashier processing a large order, the order is split among several counters. More counters can increase throughput, but too many can create overhead or contention.","KEY TERMS\n\nStripe count = number of OSTs involved.\nStripe size = amount written before moving to the next target.\nLayout = where the file's pieces live.\n\nThese choices interact with workload characteristics.","AI CONNECTION\n\nLarge checkpoint or dataset files can benefit from parallel data paths. Small files may not benefit from aggressive striping and can increase metadata/coordination overhead.","PERFORMANCE REASONING\n\nIf a file uses one target, aggregate storage bandwidth may be limited by that target. More stripes can expose more targets—but the network, clients or servers may become the next bottleneck.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nUneven target load can indicate workload/layout imbalance. Check whether a hot dataset concentrates traffic on a small number of OSTs. Do not assume maximum stripe count is always optimal.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: in a Lustre lab, inspect file layouts using the release-appropriate Lustre tools. Compare a single-target file with a striped file using a controlled benchmark.\n\nInterview: ‘Why can striping improve throughput, and why can excessive striping hurt?’\n\nNEXT LINK: tomorrow we troubleshoot Lustre systematically."),

    Day(39,"Lustre troubleshooting","Finding the fault domain","Lustre troubleshooting is distributed-systems troubleshooting. The same user symptom can originate at the client, network, metadata service, data service, target or underlying hardware.","FAULT TREE\n\nSLOW FILE ACCESS\n ├─ client CPU/memory\n ├─ client filesystem/client module\n ├─ network/LNet\n ├─ metadata service\n ├─ OSS/OST\n └─ media/device\n\nFirst determine whether the symptom is metadata or data I/O.","SCOPING QUESTIONS\n\nOne client or all?\nOne file or all files?\nOne OST or many?\nMetadata operations or bulk transfer?\nWhen did it begin?\nWhat changed?\n\nThese questions dramatically reduce search space.","EVIDENCE\n\nClient logs\nFilesystem health\nNetwork counters\nServer CPU/memory\nOST utilization\nDevice latency\nBenchmark results\n\nCorrelate timestamps across nodes.","PERFORMANCE\n\nUse controlled FIO/IOR-style workloads where appropriate. Compare healthy and affected paths. Measure rather than relying on ‘it feels slow’.","COMMON MISTAKE\n\nRestarting services immediately can destroy evidence. In a lab you can experiment freely; in production preserve logs, capture state and follow change procedures.","AI CONNECTION\n\nIf training stalls, determine whether GPUs are waiting for filesystem reads, whether checkpoint writes are causing pauses, or whether the issue is unrelated to storage. GPU utilization can be an important symptom, not a root cause.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: create a Lustre troubleshooting checklist with client → network → MDS → OSS/OST → device.\n\nInterview: ‘All clients report slow large-file reads but metadata is normal. What would you investigate next?’\n\nNEXT LINK: IBM Storage Scale provides another major parallel filesystem model."),

    Day(40,"GPFS / IBM Storage Scale","A second major parallel filesystem","IBM Storage Scale is a scale-out data platform based on the GPFS filesystem. It is widely associated with HPC, analytics and AI workloads and provides a useful comparison with Lustre.","CONCEPTUAL MODEL\n\nCLIENTS\n ↓\nSTORAGE SCALE CLUSTER\n ├─ metadata/control services\n └─ shared data/storage\n\nThe implementation details differ from Lustre, but the goal is similar: scalable shared data access.","ANALOGY\n\nLustre and Storage Scale are two different railway systems. Both move many passengers, but their track control, stations and operating procedures differ. Learn the architecture rather than memorizing brand names.","WHY IT MATTERS\n\nKnowing both makes you more useful in heterogeneous HPC environments. IBM Storage Scale supports high-performance file access and can integrate data across storage tiers and environments.","COMPARISON\n\nLustre → strongly associated with HPC parallel filesystem deployments and DDN EXAScaler.\nStorage Scale → IBM ecosystem, GPFS heritage, enterprise/HPC/AI data management.\n\nBoth require Linux, networking and performance skills.","ADMIN TASKS\n\nCluster health\nFilesystem availability\nCapacity\nPerformance\nNode membership\nNetwork paths\nData placement/tiering\n\nExact commands depend on the installed version and architecture.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nUse the same disciplined approach: scope the symptom, identify client versus cluster-side issue, inspect logs and metrics, then test with a controlled workload.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: read the official Storage Scale architecture documentation and draw a one-page component map. Compare it with your Lustre diagram.\n\nInterview: ‘What problem does a parallel filesystem solve that ordinary local filesystems cannot?’\n\nNEXT LINK: another HPC filesystem worth knowing is BeeGFS."),

    Day(41,"BeeGFS","Modern parallel filesystem concepts","BeeGFS is a parallel filesystem designed for scalable HPC and technical computing. Learning its concepts reinforces the general pattern of distributing metadata and data services.","ARCHITECTURE\n\nCLIENTS\n ↓\nMETADATA SERVICE(S)\n +\nSTORAGE SERVICE(S)\n ↓\nSTORAGE TARGETS\n\nThe names and implementation differ from Lustre, but parallelism remains central.","ANALOGY\n\nA library chain has several catalog desks and many storage rooms. Requests can be distributed instead of forcing every customer through one desk.","WHY LEARN IT?\n\nHPC employers may use multiple parallel filesystem technologies. The transferable skills are architecture, Linux, networking, metadata/data behavior and performance engineering.","PERFORMANCE\n\nParallel clients can generate aggregate throughput. Metadata-heavy workloads and bulk streaming workloads exercise different services. Storage target balance matters.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nAsk the same questions: one node or many? metadata or data? one target or many? network or storage? Compare healthy and unhealthy clients.","CAREER CONNECTION\n\nA senior HPC storage engineer should be able to learn a new parallel filesystem quickly. Understanding design patterns is more valuable than knowing only one vendor's command syntax.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: draw BeeGFS next to Lustre and mark the equivalent roles.\n\nInterview: ‘What concepts transfer from Lustre to BeeGFS?’\n\nYOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO: recognize the common architecture of parallel filesystems even when product names change."),

    Day(42,"Ceph","Distributed software-defined storage","Ceph is a distributed storage platform providing object, block and file interfaces. It is important because modern infrastructure often uses software-defined storage rather than a traditional proprietary array.","CORE IDEA\n\nCLIENTS\n ↓\nCEPH CLUSTER\n ├─ MON → cluster maps/state\n ├─ MGR → management/telemetry\n ├─ OSDs → data storage\n └─ gateways/filesystem services as required\n\nData is distributed across the cluster.","ANALOGY\n\nInstead of one warehouse with a central controller, imagine a city of warehouses where software decides where parcels belong and how to recover when one warehouse fails.","INTERFACES\n\nRBD → block\nRGW → S3/object\nCephFS → file\n\nOne platform can therefore serve multiple workload types.","WHY HPC ENGINEERS CARE\n\nCeph teaches distributed storage fundamentals: placement, replication, erasure coding, failure domains, recovery, network dependency and performance trade-offs.","PERFORMANCE\n\nOSD media, network, CPU, replication/EC overhead and client behavior all contribute. Distributed storage performance cannot be understood by looking at one disk alone.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nCheck cluster health first, then placement/data services, network, client behavior and media. Recovery/backfill can consume resources and change application performance.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: use your Ceph training lab to create a small cluster, inspect health and create an RBD or object test workload.\n\nInterview: ‘How is Ceph different from a traditional storage array?’\n\nNEXT LINK: tomorrow we go deeper into NVMe, the media technology behind modern high-performance storage."),

    Day(43,"NVMe deep dive","Queues, namespaces and parallelism","NVMe was designed for solid-state media and exposes many queues and parallel I/O capabilities. It is a key building block for AI/HPC storage systems.","CONCEPTUAL PATH\n\nAPPLICATION\n ↓\nFILESYSTEM / BLOCK LAYER\n ↓\nNVMe DRIVER\n ↓\nNVMe CONTROLLER\n ↓\nNAMESPACE\n ↓\nNAND / SSD MEDIA\n\nNVMe reduces protocol overhead and supports high concurrency.","ANALOGY\n\nAn old single-lane road becomes a multi-lane highway with many independent queues. The goal is to let many CPU cores submit work without one central queue becoming the bottleneck.","TERMS\n\nController = device interface.\nNamespace = logical block address space.\nQueue pair = submission + completion queues.\nPCIe = common local transport.\n\nLearn these before NVMe-oF.","TOOLS\n\nnvme list\nnvme id-ctrl <device>\nlsblk\ncat /sys/class/nvme/*/model\n\nExact availability depends on Linux distribution and installed nvme-cli package.","AI CONNECTION\n\nLocal NVMe can stage datasets, caches and temporary files close to GPUs. It can provide very low latency compared with remote storage, but capacity and sharing differ from a parallel filesystem.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nCheck PCIe/device visibility, kernel messages, controller health and filesystem state. A missing namespace is different from a filesystem mount problem.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: on a Linux system with NVMe, run nvme list and lsblk and map controller → namespace → filesystem.\n\nInterview: ‘Why is NVMe useful for AI infrastructure?’\n\nNEXT LINK: tomorrow we move NVMe from inside the server to across the network."),

    Day(44,"NVMe-oF transports","NVMe over a network","NVMe over Fabrics extends the NVMe command model across a fabric so hosts can access remote NVMe storage. Important transports include NVMe/TCP, NVMe/RDMA and NVMe/FC.","ARCHITECTURE\n\nHOST\nNVMe initiator\n ↓\nFABRIC\n ↓\nTARGET\nNVMe subsystem\n ↓\nNVMe media\n\nThe transport changes; the NVMe command model remains central.","TRANSPORTS\n\nNVMe/TCP → Ethernet/TCP, broad compatibility.\nNVMe/RDMA → low-latency RDMA fabrics.\nNVMe/FC → Fibre Channel environments.\n\nThis connects directly to your SAN background.","ANALOGY\n\nNVMe is the language of the request. TCP, RDMA and Fibre Channel are different roads used to carry that language to the remote storage.","LATENCY TRADE-OFF\n\nLocal NVMe avoids the network. NVMe-oF adds a network path but enables shared/disaggregated resources. High-speed fabrics reduce transport overhead and latency.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nSeparate discovery/connectivity from I/O performance. Check host NIC/HBA, routes/fabric, target availability, subsystem/namespace state and filesystem. For RDMA transports, validate RDMA health separately.","CAREER CONNECTION\n\nNVMe-oF bridges traditional SAN knowledge and modern AI storage. FC experience is valuable, but you must add TCP, RDMA/RoCE and InfiniBand concepts.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: draw NVMe/TCP, NVMe/RDMA and NVMe/FC side by side and identify their network/fabric dependencies.\n\nInterview: ‘Why might an organization choose NVMe-oF instead of local NVMe?’\n\nNEXT LINK: RDMA provides the high-performance transport used by many AI/HPC systems."),

    Day(45,"RDMA fundamentals","Moving data with less CPU overhead","RDMA enables direct memory-to-memory data movement with low latency and high throughput. It is a foundational technology behind many high-performance AI/HPC networks.","BASIC MODEL\n\nAPPLICATION\n ↓\nRDMA API\n ↓\nHCA/NIC\n ↓\nRDMA FABRIC\n ↓\nREMOTE HCA/NIC\n ↓\nREMOTE MEMORY\n\nThe design minimizes involvement of the traditional kernel network data path for the data transfer.","ANALOGY\n\nTraditional delivery asks a central receptionist to handle every package. RDMA is like giving authorized couriers a direct loading-dock route between two rooms.","TERMS\n\nRDMA = Remote Direct Memory Access\nHCA = Host Channel Adapter in InfiniBand terminology\nQP = Queue Pair\nCQ = Completion Queue\nRoCE = RDMA over Converged Ethernet\nInfiniBand = dedicated high-performance fabric\n\nThese terms will recur constantly in AI infrastructure.","WHY STORAGE CARES\n\nRDMA can carry storage traffic such as NVMe/RDMA and parallel filesystem traffic. Lower latency and CPU overhead can improve the efficiency of high-performance data paths.","AI CONNECTION\n\nGPU clusters exchange data at enormous rates. Storage and compute networks must keep GPUs supplied with data. RDMA is one part of the architecture enabling high-performance communication.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nCheck link/fabric health, HCA/NIC state, IP or fabric configuration, RDMA device visibility, counters and application-level errors. RoCE environments also require careful Ethernet configuration and congestion management.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: on a Linux host with RDMA hardware/lab access, inspect RDMA devices and record the HCA/NIC information. Without hardware, map the RDMA data path on paper.\n\nInterview: ‘What problem does RDMA solve compared with ordinary TCP networking?’\n\nCHECKPOINT: You have now connected enterprise storage → Linux → performance → parallel filesystems → NVMe → RDMA. Next block goes deeper into high-speed fabrics and AI infrastructure."),
)
